A numerical eigensolver, however, returns eigenvectors $w_n$ of unit \emph{Euclidean} length, $\sum_jw_{jn}^2=1$. (We write $w$, not $v$, for these eigenvectors, because $v_i$ will denote singular vectors in Section~\ref{sec:fitting-svd}.) The two are related by $\psi_n(x_j)=w_{jn}/\sqrt h$, so
\begin{equation}
A_{nk}=\sum_j\frac{w_{jn}^2}{h}\,\phi_k(x_j)\,h=\sum_jw_{jn}^2\,\phi_k(x_j).

 This is called \emph{stationary-phase} reasoning. A small example shows why the stationary point dominates. With a phase that never stands still, $\int_{-X}^{X}\cos(ax)\,\dd x=\bigl[\sin(ax)/a\bigr]_{-X}^{X}=2\sin(aX)/a$, at most $2/a$ in size: the lobes cancel and only an endpoint remnant of order $1/a$ survives. With the phase $ax^2$, which is stationary at $x=0$, the integrand $\cos(ax^2)$ stays positive throughout $ax^2<\pi/2$, a central interval of width $2\sqrt{\pi/2a}$ that shrinks only like $1/\sqrt a$; this uncancelled region contributes an amount of order $1/\sqrt a$, far more than $1/a$ when $a$ is large. For $a=100$ and $X=3$ the first integral is at most $0.02$ in size, while numerically the second is about $0.13$.

\begin{beyond}
The infinite-range value $\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\cos(ax^2)\,\dd x=\sqrt{\pi/2a}$ (about $0.125$ for $a=100$) is a Fresnel integral, and the general statement that an oscillatory integral is dominated by its points of stationary phase is the method of stationary phase. We take both on trust; they are treated with the method of steepest descents in \cite{rhb}.
\end{beyond}

